===== docs/plans/2026-10-06-csat-rating.md =====
# Customer Satisfaction Rating Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Let a customer rate a resolved ticket 1-5 from the portal, alert the assigned agent on a low score, produce a nightly per-agent weekly summary, and rename `assignee_id` to `owner_id` everywhere.

**Architecture:** Ratings are a new `ratings` Map in the in-memory store (same shape as the existing `tickets`/`messages` Maps), with a `rateTicket` portal function next to the existing `reply`. A new nightly job reads all ratings and writes a summary JSON file, following the exact pattern of the existing `export-tickets` job. The field rename touches every in-repo reference directly; the one external consumer (finance's export file) gets a dual-write instead of a migration phase.

**Tech Stack:** Node.js (`node --test`), no new dependencies.

---

## Decisions and recommendations

The user is away until 2026-10-07 and asked that open questions be answered with a recommendation baked into the plan, not asked interactively. These are the judgment calls this plan makes. Flag any of them if you'd choose differently when you review — the plan proceeds on these as-is.

1. **Who does a rating "belong to"?** The rating record stores its own `agent_id`, snapshotted from the ticket's assignee at the moment of rating — not looked up live from the ticket at report time. *Why:* `assignTicket` only refuses on a `closed` ticket (`src/tickets.js:27`), so a resolved ticket can be reassigned after it's rated. A live lookup at summary time would then credit/blame the wrong agent for old feedback. Snapshotting also means the Phase 3 rename can't accidentally change what a rating is attributed to.
2. **Unassigned ticket, low score.** If a resolved ticket was never assigned an agent and gets rated 1 or 2, no notification is sent (silently skipped, not an error). *Why:* this mirrors the existing guard in `reopenTicket` (`src/tickets.js:42`, `if (t.assignee_id && ...)`), and `notifyAgent` throws if called with no agent id (`src/notify.js:7`).
3. **Error style.** Invalid score, wrong ticket status, and duplicate rating all *throw* (matches `resolveTicket`/`reopenTicket`'s style of throwing on a precondition failure). Missing ticket and wrong-organisation both *return `null`* (matches `getMyTicket`/`reply`'s style, and directly satisfies the acceptance criterion that both cases get "the same answer").
4. **7-day window boundary.** A rating is excluded from the weekly summary once it is 7 days old or older (i.e. included while `age < 7 days`). *Why:* the spec pins the 8-day-excluded case but not the exactly-7-day case; this is the more conservative reading of "the past seven days" and the plan's test for this deliberately avoids asserting the exact boundary, so it stays correct either way if you'd rather include day 7.
5. **Agents with zero ratings, and null-owner ratings.** The weekly summary only lists agents who have at least one rating in the window; a rating on a ticket that was never assigned (no agent to credit) is excluded from the grouping entirely. *Why:* there's no agent registry anywhere in this codebase to enumerate "all agents" against, so listing zero-rating agents isn't possible without inventing one — out of scope for this spec.
6. **Naming.** Job file is `src/jobs/summarize-ratings.js` (function `summarizeRatings`), output is `exports/rating-summary.json`, test is `test/summarize-ratings.test.js`. *Why:* matches the existing verb-first job naming (`close-resolved.js`, `purge-closed.js`, `export-tickets.js`).
7. **Rename scope.** The rename is expand-only everywhere in this repo (`src/tickets.js`, `src/portal.js`, `test/tickets.test.js`, `test/export-tickets.test.js`, `docs/glossary.md`) — not a 3-phase expand/migrate/contract. *Why:* it's a small, fully-controlled, atomic change; a partial rename would break tests immediately so it can't land half-done. The one place this isn't safe is `exports/tickets.json`, read by finance's own reporting job outside this repo (confirmed by the comment at `src/jobs/export-tickets.js:3`) — that file dual-writes both `owner_id` and the legacy `assignee_id` key with the same value. Dropping the legacy key later needs finance's confirmation and is explicitly out of scope here.
8. **Docs.** `docs/glossary.md`'s "Assignee" heading becomes "Owner", per the spec's own "everywhere" wording.

